.TH HD-SECBOOT 7 "October 2026" "hd-base" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-secboot \- the hyprdebian Secure Boot keys
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-base
lets a hyprdebian system boot under Secure Boot with keys of its owner,
instead of the keys of Microsoft that most firmware ships with.
.BR hd-secboot (8)
generates the keys, writes them in the firmware, turns Secure Boot on and
off, and adds the hashes of option ROMs to db.
.PP
It depends on the tools that make and use the keys, so they are installed
with it and stay installed:
.TP
.BR openssl (1)
makes the keys and their certificates;
.TP
.B efitools
turns the certificates into EFI signature lists and signed updates, and
enrolls them with
.BR efi-updatevar (1);
.TP
.B sbsigntool
signs EFI files with
.BR sbsign (1)
and checks them with
.BR sbverify (1).
.PP
On its first installation, it generates the keys in
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot
with
.BR "hd-secboot \-\-generate" .
When that directory already exists, on an upgrade, a reinstall or when the
keys were put there by hand, the keys are kept: keys that are enrolled in
the firmware are never replaced.
Removing or purging the package keeps the keys as well.
.SH THE KEYS
There are three keys, all with the same owner GUID, a random UUID in
.IR GUID.txt :
.TP
.B PK
the platform key, which owns the firmware and signs updates of KEK;
.TP
.B KEK
the key exchange key, which signs updates of db;
.TP
.B db
the signature database key, which signs the EFI files that may boot.
.PP
Each key is RSA 2048, the size that the UEFI specification requires every
firmware to support, with a self-signed certificate that is valid for ten
years and named
.IR "HyprDebian <hostname> Secure Boot <key>" .
For each key
.IR <key> ,
the directory holds:
.TP
.I <key>.key
the private key;
.TP
.I <key>.crt
the certificate as PEM, for
.BR sbsign ;
.TP
.I <key>.cer
the certificate as DER, for the key menus of firmware setups;
.TP
.I <key>.esl
the certificate as EFI signature list;
.TP
.I <key>.auth
the signature list as signed update, for
.B efi-updatevar
or KeyTool: PK signs its own and that of KEK, KEK signs that of db.
.PP
The directory and the files are only readable by root.
The keys are generated in
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot.tmp
and moved into place when they are all there, so a failed installation never
leaves half a set behind.
.SH ENROLLING THE KEYS
Put the firmware in setup mode, which removes its platform key, in its
setup menu.
Most firmware has an entry under Secure Boot to delete the platform key, to
clear all keys, or to reset to setup mode.
Then write db and KEK, which replaces what they hold, and PK last, since
that ends setup mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hd-secboot \-\-store
hd-secboot \-\-enable
.fi
.RE
.PP
Some firmware does not let the system write the keys; enroll the
.I .auth
or
.I .cer
files from its setup menu instead.
.PP
Sign the EFI files that are to boot with the db key before Secure Boot is
turned on;
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8)
does that for the bootloader and the kernels:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sbsign \-\-key db.key \-\-cert db.crt \-\-output signed.efi unsigned.efi
.fi
.RE
.PP
After the next boot,
.B hd-secboot \-\-status
says whether Secure Boot is on; when it is not, turn it on in the setup menu
of the firmware.
.SH OPTION ROMS
Some devices have a driver in their option ROM that the firmware loads before
the system boots, such as the driver (GOP) that a graphics card needs to
show anything before the kernel starts.
Those drivers are signed with a certificate of Microsoft, which db does not
hold, so with Secure Boot on the firmware refuses them: the setup menu of the
firmware and the bootloader menu stay black.
.B hd-secboot \-\-append\-rom\-hash
adds the hashes of exactly those drivers to db instead, so no other image
signed by Microsoft can boot.
.SH TURNING SECURE BOOT OFF
.B hd-secboot \-\-disable
removes the platform key, which puts the firmware back in setup mode, so
Secure Boot is off from the next boot, without the setup menu.
db and KEK keep what they hold;
.B hd-secboot \-\-enable
turns it on again.
.B hd-secboot \-\-clear
empties db and KEK as well.
.SH BACKUP
Back up
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot
somewhere safe.
Without the private keys, no new EFI file can be signed for a firmware that
has the keys enrolled; with them, anyone can.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/secboot
The keys.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-base (7),
.BR hd-secboot (8),
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8),
